--TEST--
Core\Arr::reduce offers its callback the key as a third argument, which array_reduce cannot pass and which a PHP callback declaring it turns into an ArgumentCountError
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`array_reduce` calls its callback with exactly two arguments, `($carry, $item)`, and a PHP callback declaring a third gets `ArgumentCountError: Too few arguments … 2 passed and exactly 3 expected` — so a fold over an array's keys has no `array_reduce` spelling at all and is written with `foreach` instead. `Core\Arr::reduce` passes `($carry, $value, $key)`, extending § 2's "every callback receives `($value, $key)`" with a leading carry rather than departing from it; `nvs_core_arr_reduce`'s doc comment owns that reasoning, and R9 is what lets the two-parameter form stay the common shape rather than becoming an error of its own. The arity is read **once** for the whole fold, so a callback declaring two parameters is never offered a key and never builds one. The key itself is a `string` even over a list (`rule:types/arrays`) — `array_reduce` would have handed an `int`, had it handed anything. A second, smaller divergence has no output to show and so is stated only here: `$initial` is required in Novis and optional in PHP, because the seed is what binds the fold's type, where `array_reduce([1, 2, 3], fn ($c, $v) => $c + $v)` starts from `null` and coerces it to `0`. The half both languages express — every fold whose callback takes the pair — is asserted against PHP itself in `arr-reduce-matches-array_reduce`.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A list's keys, which arrive as the strings `rule:types/arrays` stores. The sentinel
// is there because the row ends in a space and a trailing space before a
// newline is not reliably asserted.
array<int> $nums = [3, 8, 5];
echo Core\Arr::reduce($nums, fn (string $c, int $v, string $k): string => $c . $k . ":" . $v . " ", ""), "|\n";

// A map, where the fold is over pairs rather than values — the case
// `array_reduce` is most often reached for and cannot answer.
array<int> $map = ["b" => 8, "a" => 2, "c" => 9];
echo Core\Arr::reduce($map, fn (string $c, int $v, string $k): string => $c . $k . "=" . $v . ";", ""), "\n";

// The same subject folded by a two-parameter callback, which is offered no key
// at all: the arity decides it once, before the walk, so this row and the one
// above differ only in what the callback declared.
echo Core\Arr::reduce($map, fn (string $c, int $v): string => $c . $v . ";", ""), "\n";

// A gapped list. The key offered is the entry's own, not its position in the
// walk, so the two removed entries leave their numbers out of the fold rather
// than closing up behind them.
array<int> $gapped = [10, 20, 30, 40];
unset($gapped[0]);
unset($gapped[2]);
echo Core\Arr::reduce($gapped, fn (string $c, int $v, string $k): string => $c . $k . ":" . $v . ";", ""), "\n";

// The key can be the whole fold, with the value taking no part: a member that
// passed the value twice, or the key second, prints the rows above plausibly
// and fails this one.
echo Core\Arr::reduce($map, fn (string $c, int $v, string $k): string => $c . $k, ""), "\n";

// The empty subject reaches the callback zero times whatever its arity, so the
// seed comes back and no key is built.
array<int> $empty = [];
echo Core\Arr::reduce($empty, fn (string $c, int $v, string $k): string => $c . $k, "seed"), "\n";

// The half that does not diverge, on the same member: the two-parameter fold,
// asserted against PHP itself in the matching case named above.
echo Core\Arr::reduce($nums, fn (int $c, int $v): int => $c + $v, 0), "\n";
--EXPECT--
0:3 1:8 2:5 |
b=8;a=2;c=9;
8;2;9;
1:20;3:40;
bac
seed
16
